   - Network Design Notes: Switches, Multilayer Switches, and Tiered Architectures
   - i want to connect more devices than a 24-port switch can handle, so i add another switch and interconnect both to a router — but i want to avoid a single point of failure
     that’s exactly where multilayer switches and tiered network design come into play
     Switch	                  - L2	      - connects devices within the same network using MAC addresses
     Router	                  - L3	      - connects different networks using IP addresses (LAN ↔ WAN)
     Multilayer Switch (MLS)	- L3 + L2	- routes and switches internally, handles both IP (L3) and MAC (L2) traffic

   - a multilayer switch is used when,
     you have multiple VLANs or multiple switches that need to communicate internally at Layer 3
     you need high-speed routing without sending traffic to a separate router
     you want to keep internal traffic within your LAN for better performance and lower latency
     real-world use: Inside large buildings, campuses, or enterprises where VLAN-to-VLAN communication is common and performance is critical
     
   - VLAN (Virtual Local Area Network)
     VLAN stands for Virtual Local Area Network,
     it's a logical grouping of devices within a larger physical network, allowing devices to communicate as if they are on the same local network, even if they are on different switches
     you use subnetting to assign IP ranges to VLANs, but VLANs themselves are created by configuring switch ports—not by creating subnets

   - VLANs are used to,
     segment a network into smaller parts
     improve security
     reduce broadcast traffic
     separate departments or functions (e.g HR, Finance, IT)

   # Network Architecture Designs
     - 2-Tier Network Architecture (Collapsed Core)
       - Access Layer:
            regular Layer 2 switches
            connects to user devices (PCs, printers, etc.)
       - Distribution Layer:
            multilayer switches (Layer 3)
            handles,
               inter-VLAN routing
               route filtering
               ACLs and IPS policies
               routing protocols
               redundancy
       - also connected to routers for external access (e.g., internet)
       - core functions are often collapsed into this layer in small/medium networks — this is why it's called "Collapsed Core"
       - best for: Small to medium networks
      
     - 3-Tier Network Architecture
       - Access Layer
            same as in 2-tier
       - Distribution Layer
            still includes multilayer switches
            handles local traffic policies and aggregation
       - Core Layer
            dedicated high-speed Layer 3 switches (core switches)
            provides fast, redundant backbone routing
            focus is on speed, reliability, and low latency
            connects all distribution switches and routes them toward the data center, firewall, or external routers
       - best for: Large enterprise/campus networks
      
   # Types of Ports in Networking Switches
     - Access Port
       an access port connects to end devices like PCs, printers, IP phones
       it carries traffic for only one VLAN (untagged)
       the switch assigns all incoming and outgoing traffic on that port to a specific VLAN
       use Case:
         assigning a desktop computer to VLAN 10
         simplifies configuration because the port only belongs to one VLAN
     - Trunk Port
       a trunk port carries traffic for multiple VLANs
       frames are tagged with VLAN IDs using protocols like 802.1Q
       used to connect switches to other switches, or to routers (for inter-VLAN routing)
       use Case:
          connecting two switches, each with multiple VLANs
          connecting a switch to a router (Router-on-a-Stick setup)
     - Console Port
       the console port is used for local management of the switch or router
       it provides out-of-band access to configure the device using a serial connection
       you typically connect using a console cable and software like PuTTY or Tera Term
       use Case:
         initial setup of a switch or router
         troubleshooting when remote access is down
         not used for network traffic
         only accessible physically (or via console server)
     - Management Port (Out-of-Band Management)
       a management port is a dedicated ethernet port for managing the device out-of-band
       it’s separated from the data network and used only for administration
       use Case:
         managing the switch from a different network than the one it's switching
         adding an extra layer of security by isolating management traffic
       difference from Console Port:
         console port uses serial (local)
         management port uses Ethernet (can be remote)

