   # Wide Area Networks (WAN) and Enterprise Connectivity
     Local Area Networks (LANs) : internal networks within corporate sites or data centers
     Wide Area Networks  (WANs) : interconnect geographically dispersed sites like branch offices, corporate headquarters, and data centers
                                  WANs facilitate communication beyond the boundaries of LANs

   # Traditional WAN Technologies
     leased Lines:
     point-to-point dedicated circuits leased from carriers to connect corporate offices, data centers, and branch sites
     common Types:
        T1 (1.54 Mbps)
        T3 (~43 Mbps)
        E1 (European equivalent of T1)
        E3 (European equivalent of T3)

      pros:
      consistent, private bandwidth
      guaranteed latency and performance
      
      Cons:
      expensive and inflexible
      difficult to scale across multiple sites

   # MPLS ( Multiprotocol Label Switching )
     a widely adopted WAN technology since the 1990s, still prevalent today
     how it Works,
     uses labels instead of IP addresses for packet forwarding
     provides VPN-like services over shared infrastructure
     operates at Layer 2.5 (between Data Link and Network layers)
     MPLS VPN: Logically separate traffic over shared MPLS network (not inherently encrypted)

     pros:
     connects multiple sites using a single link into the provider's MPLS cloud
     simplifies WAN management
     offers Quality of Service (QoS) for prioritizing traffic (e.g., VoIP, video conferencing)
     logical separation of customer traffic through label switching

     cons:
     higher cost
     complexity in setup and management

     devices terminology:
     CE (Customer Edge) Router : at the customer site
     PE (Provider Edge) Router : at the provider’s network, performs MPLS operations

   # Metro-E ( Metro Ethernet ) 
     provides ethernet-based point-to-point or multipoint connectivity in metropolitan areas
     key characteristics,
        operates at Layer 2
        extends LAN across wide areas
        fiber optic-based infrastructure
        high bandwidth: 1 Gbps, 10 Gbps, or more

      service Models,
        E-Line : point-to-point           (like leased line but Ethernet-based)
                 E-Line is a point-to-point Ethernet Virtual Connection (EVC) between two customer locations
                 it’s like a leased line, but uses Ethernet technology
                 best for 2-site connections, Simple, private, high-performance
        E-LAN  : multipoint-to-multipoint (shared LAN-like environment)
                 a dedicated fiber cable is used to connect the user (customer site) to the provider's facilitya
                 at the provider’s end, this connects to a special switch (called a virtual switch), which is a physical switch already installed by the company at various locations
                 the same setup is used at the other office site as well
                 best when all sites need to talk to each other — like a big virtual LAN
        E-Tree : hub-and-spoke            (central site communicates with branches)
                 E-Tree is a type of ethernet service used to connect multiple locations, like a central office (hub) and several branches (spokes)
                 best when you want a central hub talking to many isolated branches

      use case,
      commonly used for high-speed connections between offices/data centers in the same city
      cons:
      higher cost
      less suitable for connecting widely dispersed branches

   # Public Internet with Site-to-Site VPNs
     creates encrypted tunnels over the public internet
     connects branch offices securely to corporate networks
     protects data from interception on untrusted networks
     internet-based Site-to-Site VPN: Adds encryption and security

     pros:
     low cost
     flexible and easy to deploy

     cons:
     inconsistent performance due to public internet variability
     lack of robust QoS, which can impact services like VoIP
     encryption overhead and additional configuration complexity

     note:
     end-user devices at branch sites don’t require VPN clients. VPNs are managed at the router/firewall level

   # SD-WAN ( Software-Defined WAN )
     a modern solution that uses software-defined networking to manage WAN connectivity
     how it works,
     uses public internet, MPLS, and other transport types
     creates a virtual overlay network with centralized control
     routes traffic intelligently based on application performance, path health, and policy

     pros:   
     lower cost by utilizing internet links
     improved traffic management, QoS, and application performance
     enhanced security through built-in encryption and segmentation
     direct cloud access from branch sites (e.g., AWS, Azure)

     impact on WAN design:
     reduces dependence on data center hubs
     matches the modern cloud-first architecture

       



